.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt FIREWALL 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm firewall
.Nd simple firewalls with pf
.Sh FIREWALL BASICS
A Firewall is most commonly used to protect an internal network
from an outside network by preventing the outside network from
making arbitrary connections into the internal network.
Firewalls
are also used to prevent outside entities from spoofing internal
IP addresses and to isolate services such as NFS or SMBFS (Windows
file sharing) within LAN segments.
.Pp
The 5BSD packet filter,
.Xr pf 4
from
.Ox ,
can also limit bandwidth by passing packets through
.Xr dummynet 4
pipes and queues.
This feature can be useful when you need to guarantee a certain
amount of bandwidth for a critical purpose.
For example, if you
are doing video conferencing over the Internet via your
office T1 (1.5 MBits/s), you may wish to bandwidth-limit all other
T1 traffic to 1 MBit/s in order to reserve at least 0.5 MBits
for your video conferencing connections.
Similarly if you are
running a popular web or ftp site from a colocation facility
you might want to limit bandwidth to prevent excessive bandwidth
charges from your provider.
.Pp
Finally,
.Xr pf 4
translates addresses and ports itself
.Po Cm nat-to , rdr-to , binat-to , af-to Pc ,
changes the next hop of packets
.Po Cm route-to , reply-to Pc ,
and hands packets to local programs
.Pq Cm divert-to , divert-packet ; No see Xr divert 4 .
NAT (network address translation) allows an internal network using
a private IP space to make connections to the outside for browsing
or other purposes.
.Pp
Constructing a firewall may appear to be trivial, but most people
get them wrong.
The most common mistake is to create an exclusive
firewall rather than an inclusive firewall.
An exclusive firewall
allows all packets through except for those matching a set of rules.
An inclusive firewall allows only packets matching the ruleset
through.
Inclusive firewalls are much, much safer than exclusive
firewalls but a tad more difficult to build properly.
The
second most common mistake is to blackhole everything except the
particular port you want to let through.
TCP/IP needs to be able
to get certain types of ICMP errors to function properly - for
example, to implement MTU discovery.
Also, a number of common
system daemons make reverse connections to the
.Sy auth
service in an attempt to authenticate the user making a connection.
Auth is rather dangerous but the proper implementation is to return
a TCP reset for the connection attempt rather than simply blackholing
the packet.
We cover these and other quirks involved with constructing
a firewall in the sample firewall below.
.Sh ENABLING PF
5BSD's packet filter is
.Ox
PF; the
.Fx
firewalls ipfw, natd and IPFilter are not part of the system.
.Pa pf.ko ,
which also contains the
.Xr pflog 4 ,
.Xr pfsync 4
and
.Xr pflow 4
interfaces, is preloaded by
.Xr loader 8
.Po
.Va pf_load
in
.Xr loader.conf 5
.Pc ,
or can be built into the kernel with
.Cd "device pf" .
After boot, kernel modules are loaded only through the SystemExtension
broker
.Pq Xr BSDExtension 8 ;
the
.Xr rc 8
scripts do that when pf is not preloaded.
.Pp
Loading pf does not filter anything: until a ruleset is loaded and pf is
enabled, every packet passes, and a ruleset passes whatever no rule
matches.
Enable pf and the logging daemon in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pf_enable="YES"
pf_rules="/etc/pf.conf"
pflog_enable="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
Check a ruleset before loading it, and load it with
.Xr pfctl 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pfctl -nf /etc/pf.conf
# pfctl -f /etc/pf.conf
.Ed
.Pp
If the ruleset in
.Va pf_rules
fails to load at boot,
.Va pf_fallback_rules_enable
loads a fallback ruleset
.Po
.Dq block drop log all
by default
.Pc ,
so a broken file does not leave the machine open; see
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
Test a new ruleset from the console, not over a connection it may block.
.Pp
Each
.Xr jail 8
with its own network stack
.Pq Cm vnet
runs its own pf, with its own rules, states and tables, configured from
inside the jail.
.Sh SAMPLE PF FIREWALL
This example is a gateway with three interfaces:
.Li em0
faces the Internet,
.Li em1
serves the internal LAN 10.0.2.0/24, and
.Li em2
serves a DMZ, 10.0.3.0/24, holding a web server at 10.0.3.10.
The LAN and DMZ are isolated from the Internet and from each other: the
LAN may reach the DMZ and the Internet, the DMZ may reach only the
Internet, and the Internet may reach only the gateway's SSH service and,
through a redirection, the web server.
Outgoing connections from both networks are translated to the address of
.Li em0 .
Forwarding must be enabled with
.Va gateway_enable
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Pp
pf evaluates the whole ruleset for the first packet of a connection and
the last matching rule decides, unless a rule says
.Cm quick .
Passed connections create state, so replies are passed without further
rules.
The
.Cm antispoof
rule blocks packets that claim an internal source address on the wrong
interface; you must make sure that your Internet router protects your
exposed networks from outside spoofing.
The
.Cm block return
rule answers
.Sy auth
connections with a TCP reset, and the ICMP rules let path MTU discovery
and IPv6 neighbor discovery work.
.Bd -literal
# /etc/pf.conf
ext_if = "em0"
int_if = "em1"
dmz_if = "em2"
lan = "10.0.2.0/24"
dmz = "10.0.3.0/24"
www = "10.0.3.10"

set skip on lo0
set block-policy drop

match in all scrub (no-df random-id)
match out on $ext_if inet from { $lan $dmz } nat-to ($ext_if:0)

block log all
antispoof quick for { $int_if $dmz_if }
block return in quick on $ext_if proto tcp to port auth

pass inet proto icmp icmp-type { echoreq unreach }
pass inet6 proto icmp6

pass in on $ext_if proto tcp to ($ext_if) port ssh
pass in on $ext_if proto tcp to ($ext_if) port www rdr-to $www
pass out on $dmz_if proto tcp to $www port www

pass in on $int_if from $lan
pass out on $dmz_if from $lan
pass in on $dmz_if from $dmz to ! $lan
pass out on $ext_if
.Ed
.Pp
Blocked packets are logged to
.Xr pflog 4
and written by
.Xr pflogd 8
to
.Pa /var/log/pflog ;
read them with
.Dl # tcpdump -n -e -ttt -r /var/log/pflog
and watch the ruleset and its counters with
.Dl # pfctl -s rules -v
.Sh TRAFFIC SHAPING
Bandwidth limits use
.Xr dummynet 4
objects configured with
.Xr dnctl 8
and selected by the
.Cm dnpipe
and
.Cm dnqueue
rule options of
.Xr pf.conf 5 .
To limit web traffic from the LAN in the example above to 1 Mbit/s, put
.Dl pipe 1 config bw 1Mbit/s
in
.Pa /etc/dnctl.conf ,
set
.Va dnctl_enable Ns = Ns Qq YES
in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf ,
and add a last rule to
.Pa /etc/pf.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pass out on $ext_if proto tcp from $lan to port { www https } \e
    dnpipe 1
.Ed
.Pp
pf's own bandwidth queueing
.Pq the Cm queue No keyword
is not supported on 5BSD yet, and rulesets that define queues are refused.
.Sh PORT BINDING INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL SERVICES
Filtering is not the only protection for services on a firewall host.
When you have a
host with multiple IP addresses assigned to it, you can bind services run
on that host to specific IPs or interfaces rather than all IPs.
Take
the gateway in the example above: it is known by its Internet address on
em0, by 10.0.2.1 on the LAN and by 10.0.3.1 on the DMZ.
If the gateway provides file sharing services to the LAN, you can use
samba's 'bind interfaces' directive to bind them to just 10.0.2.1, so they
are not offered to the DMZ or the Internet even if a rule is wrong.
The same goes for NFS.
If the LAN has your UNIX engineering
workstations, you can tell nfsd to bind specifically to 10.0.2.1.
You
can specify how to bind virtually every service on the machine and you
can use a light
.Xr jail 8
to indirectly bind services that do not otherwise give you the option.
